 The original playbook might have had some formatting issues, like missing colons or incorrect indentation.\n\nSo, the corrected playbook should have two tasks. The first task retrieves node names and registers them. The second task deletes nodes, using the \'node_names\' variable directly for the nodes parameter, without the \'stdout_lines\' part.\n\nI also need to make sure that the \'nodes\' parameter is correctly formatted as a list. If \'node_names\' is a list, then assigning it directly should work. If it\'s a string, maybe splitting it would be necessary, but in this case, I think the first task\'s output is a list, so it should be fine.\n\nAnother thing to check is whether the kubectl module is correctly specified. The original task used \'ansible.kubernetes.core.kubectl\', which is correct. But perhaps the way the arguments are passed needs adjustment. Using \'args\' as a list with each argument separately might be better.\n\nWait, in the original playbook, the \'args\' are passed as a list, which is correct. So that part is okay.\n\nIn summary, the main fixes are:\n\n1. Remove the \'vars\' section in the second task and use \'node_names\' directly in the \'nodes\' parameter.\n2. Ensure proper YAML syntax and indentation.\n3. Verify that the variable is correctly referenced without unnecessary parts like \'.stdout_lines\'.\n\nBy making these changes, the playbook should no longer cause templating errors and should pass ansible-lint.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Restore etcd - get all nodes\n  ansible.kubernetes.core.kubectl:\n    command: get\n    args:\n      - nodes\n      - -o\n      - custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name\n      - --no-headers\n    register: node_names\n\n- name: Restore etcd - remove old nodes\n  ansible.kubernetes.core.kubectl:\n    command: delete\n    args:\n      - nodes\n      - --ignore-not-found=true\n    nodes: "{{ node_names }}"\n```' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'deepseek-r1:14b', 'created_at': '2025-10